WEBVTT

00:00:15.970 --> 00:00:17.890
Are these things stupid or what?

00:00:17.970 --> 00:00:19.430
Just like an albatross.

00:00:19.640 --> 00:00:21.930
Oh, eggs! Fresh eggs!

00:00:22.940 --> 00:00:25.400
We called them "fool birds"
because they were so easy to catch.

00:00:25.690 --> 00:00:27.520
But they almost went extinct
from overhunting.

00:00:28.150 --> 00:00:30.690
What are you trying to say
with that bit of trivia?

00:00:31.030 --> 00:00:33.030
I'm saying we can't let it happen again.

00:00:33.400 --> 00:00:35.410
All right, I'll put two back.

00:00:37.490 --> 00:00:40.040
If it won't rain heavily
tomorrow morning, let's keep moving.

00:00:40.240 --> 00:00:42.580
Okay! Where to? Let's explore!

00:00:43.370 --> 00:00:45.540
Maybe to Australia? Or how about Hawaii?

00:00:45.870 --> 00:00:47.000
What do you think, Natsu?

00:00:47.380 --> 00:00:49.460
What? Oh, well...

00:00:50.050 --> 00:00:53.970
I think maybe it's best if we met back up
with Ms. Botan and everyone.

00:00:54.050 --> 00:00:55.880
Yes, I'm with you.

00:00:56.430 --> 00:00:58.470
I think they might be having
a hard time without us.

00:00:58.550 --> 00:01:00.850
Oh, good point.

00:01:01.060 --> 00:01:03.600
Botan is crazy strong,
but even she could use some help.

00:01:03.980 --> 00:01:06.650
All right, that settles it!
Let's head back to Kyushu!

00:01:07.270 --> 00:01:10.320
Okay, then I would like to
make a proposal.

00:01:11.020 --> 00:01:13.490
I've always played team sports.

00:01:13.900 --> 00:01:15.950
One thing we always did was set goals,

00:01:16.610 --> 00:01:19.780
three weeks, or three months,
or even up to a year in advance.

00:01:20.450 --> 00:01:23.410
It makes it easier to decide
what we have to do and when to do it,

00:01:23.500 --> 00:01:25.710
so that we get actual results.

00:01:26.540 --> 00:01:28.790
I think it's best if we
decide on some overall goals.

00:01:29.250 --> 00:01:30.170
For example,

00:01:30.250 --> 00:01:31.500
our three-week goals are

00:01:31.590 --> 00:01:34.420
to learn how to steer the ship
and to meet up with everyone.

00:01:34.920 --> 00:01:36.970
Then find a nice place to live
within three months.

00:01:37.050 --> 00:01:40.600
And finally, establish a routine
for our daily lives within a year.

00:01:42.600 --> 00:01:44.060
Sounds good to me.

00:01:48.730 --> 00:01:49.650
Natsu.

00:01:51.060 --> 00:01:52.730
Did that come as a surprise to you, too?

00:01:54.150 --> 00:01:55.940
Setting solid goals for myself.

00:01:56.280 --> 00:01:57.740
It's been a while since I did that.

00:01:58.070 --> 00:01:59.320
Since elementary school?

00:01:59.700 --> 00:02:01.950
We all used to set goals
for the summer vacation.

00:02:02.410 --> 00:02:05.790
But I never achieved them.
I bet you were the same, right?

00:02:06.200 --> 00:02:07.210
Yes.

00:02:08.500 --> 00:02:11.590
And before you know it,
everyone is at different stages in life.

00:02:12.420 --> 00:02:13.670
But it's amazing.

00:02:14.000 --> 00:02:15.550
Aren't you at least a little excited?

00:02:18.220 --> 00:02:21.010
Hey! Don't start acting so confident.

00:02:21.090 --> 00:02:22.760
Hey, you two. It's time for bed.

00:02:26.980 --> 00:02:30.350
Listen up, Arashi.
I'm not setting any goals for myself.

00:02:30.650 --> 00:02:32.360
I just want to live my life as it happens.

00:02:32.440 --> 00:02:33.690
That's fine with me.

00:02:33.770 --> 00:02:34.730
For real?

00:02:34.820 --> 00:02:36.990
I know you'll step up when it counts.

00:02:37.070 --> 00:02:38.070
You think so?

00:02:41.450 --> 00:02:44.330
-The S.S. Elephant n' Lion sets sail.
-The S.S. Elephant n' Lion sets sail.

00:04:18.460 --> 00:04:19.460
WINTER SOLSTICE

00:04:19.550 --> 00:04:21.170
<i>Haru found some footprints.</i>

00:04:21.840 --> 00:04:26.220
<i>They looked relatively fresh
and likely left by two men.</i>

00:04:31.560 --> 00:04:32.560
What?

00:04:33.140 --> 00:04:34.270
What's wrong, Haru?

00:04:34.560 --> 00:04:37.020
Oh, nothing. It's just my imagination.

00:04:37.610 --> 00:04:38.570
Okay.

00:04:39.190 --> 00:04:41.400
There's no way that could be...

00:04:46.740 --> 00:04:47.910
You scared me.

00:04:47.990 --> 00:04:49.490
I thought you were Ran and the others.

00:04:49.660 --> 00:04:51.040
Actually, who are you guys?

00:04:51.620 --> 00:04:55.040
We're members of Team Spring
and Team Winter.

00:04:56.170 --> 00:04:58.920
<i>They were Team Autumn.</i>

00:04:59.250 --> 00:05:03.300
<i>After abandoning their village,
they evacuated here a couple weeks prior.</i>

00:05:03.760 --> 00:05:06.380
The ashes ruined the crops
and everything else.

00:05:06.930 --> 00:05:09.640
As we were fleeing,
we came across this underground entrance.

00:05:10.180 --> 00:05:12.930
That means the footprints we found
must have belonged to you two.

00:05:13.640 --> 00:05:18.150
Yes, we've been walking around
to figure out where all these passages go.

00:05:18.520 --> 00:05:21.780
So tell us, why did your group split up?

00:05:21.860 --> 00:05:26.150
We passed the village you made
on the way here. It was remarkable.

00:05:26.780 --> 00:05:29.660
Akiwo and Ms. Ran made that happen.

00:05:29.910 --> 00:05:32.490
They kept it safe
by ruling with an iron fist.

00:05:34.700 --> 00:05:36.370
However, things are different now.

00:05:36.870 --> 00:05:39.000
Ms. Kurumi is pregnant.

00:05:40.460 --> 00:05:43.760
But Ms. Ran and the others insisted
that no children be born.

00:05:45.510 --> 00:05:47.220
Why can't you have children?

00:05:47.760 --> 00:05:52.850
Those two harbor a strong grudge against
the people who sent us here.

00:05:53.560 --> 00:05:58.350
Regardless of this situation,
we all should be helping each other.

00:05:58.980 --> 00:06:00.520
That's for damn sure.

00:06:01.400 --> 00:06:03.570
So what exactly did you all come here for?

00:06:03.650 --> 00:06:07.530
Oh, did any of you happen to
come across Team Summer B?

00:06:08.280 --> 00:06:09.360
Summer B?

00:06:09.570 --> 00:06:11.830
Yes, we did. But they left right away.

00:06:12.530 --> 00:06:13.830
I see.

00:06:13.910 --> 00:06:15.830
You've met that team, too?

00:06:16.410 --> 00:06:17.500
We're searching for them.

00:06:17.910 --> 00:06:19.870
Hana's boyfriend is in that group.

00:06:20.880 --> 00:06:21.960
Give it up.

00:06:22.040 --> 00:06:24.340
I'm sure he's already
gotten with another girl.

00:06:24.960 --> 00:06:27.220
I had a girlfriend too, but I've moved on.

00:06:27.300 --> 00:06:29.800
Men are all like that, you know.

00:06:30.800 --> 00:06:34.720
Not everyone in the world
has the same traits as you.

00:06:34.810 --> 00:06:38.100
Yes, speak for yourself.
Don't lump all of us men together.

00:06:41.520 --> 00:06:43.900
Okay, I think it's time
we continued on our way.

00:06:43.980 --> 00:06:46.570
The sun is about to set.
How about you stay here for the night?

00:06:57.200 --> 00:06:58.460
I knew I wasn't imagining it.

00:06:58.830 --> 00:07:00.500
I can hear it clearly at night.

00:07:01.210 --> 00:07:02.580
But it doesn't make any sense.

00:07:07.800 --> 00:07:09.470
Good job, Akane.

00:07:12.840 --> 00:07:14.350
Mind getting up for a minute?

00:07:14.430 --> 00:07:17.310
There's a place I would like to go
before the tide goes out.

00:07:17.680 --> 00:07:20.020
If you do that,
I'll make sure he gets back safe.

00:07:20.190 --> 00:07:21.230
"He"?

00:07:21.520 --> 00:07:22.730
What happened to Haru?

00:07:23.150 --> 00:07:24.860
We're keeping an eye on him.

00:07:25.190 --> 00:07:26.780
I promise no harm will come to him.

00:07:27.400 --> 00:07:28.860
You've got a boat, right?

00:07:28.940 --> 00:07:30.450
I want you to use that to scout for us.

00:07:31.910 --> 00:07:33.660
Then why didn't you just ask?

00:07:33.820 --> 00:07:35.450
You didn't need to do this to make me go.

00:07:36.280 --> 00:07:38.410
I want to know about this world,
so I'll go anywhere.

00:07:38.500 --> 00:07:40.080
I would have cooperated with you.

00:07:41.370 --> 00:07:42.920
Then I'm asking you now.

00:07:43.420 --> 00:07:46.750
It seems waking up in the future
has made you forget common courtesy.

00:07:47.920 --> 00:07:48.920
Let's go, Ms. Hana.

00:07:49.260 --> 00:07:52.220
Diamond, Battery,
let's find and save Haru.

00:07:53.260 --> 00:07:54.590
I'll join you.

00:07:54.970 --> 00:07:56.050
Mr. Izayoi.

00:07:56.760 --> 00:07:59.180
I apologize that things
have turned out like this.

00:07:59.270 --> 00:08:02.440
Despite how he looks,
Haru is actually really sensitive.

00:08:03.060 --> 00:08:05.150
If anything happens to him, you'll pay.

00:08:05.230 --> 00:08:08.440
I'm sorry. I'm really very sorry.

00:08:08.860 --> 00:08:10.030
It's fine.

00:08:10.320 --> 00:08:14.700
To be honest, I never thought
all the teams would get along.

00:08:15.240 --> 00:08:19.080
More importantly,
haven't you guys noticed that sound?

00:08:19.910 --> 00:08:23.540
I've been hearing the sound of a motor
this whole time.

00:08:23.620 --> 00:08:25.790
<i>There's a machine at work somewhere.</i>

00:08:28.130 --> 00:08:32.590
<i>Ms. Akane has a good sixth sense
and warned us it's dangerous up ahead.</i>

00:08:33.050 --> 00:08:36.340
<i>That's why Ryuusei came up with a plan
to make you go instead.</i>

00:08:37.930 --> 00:08:38.850
Ms. Hana.

00:08:43.890 --> 00:08:44.890
What the hell?

00:08:45.770 --> 00:08:47.110
We'll be taking your boat.

00:08:47.440 --> 00:08:49.940
Maybe there are some supplies up ahead?

00:08:50.570 --> 00:08:52.320
If so, we'll be taking those too.

00:08:52.860 --> 00:08:55.200
Stop! Akiwo! Ms. Ran!

00:08:55.860 --> 00:08:56.950
Where's Mr. Aramaki?

00:09:05.330 --> 00:09:06.370
Are you okay?

00:09:07.210 --> 00:09:08.920
I can't swim.

00:09:09.880 --> 00:09:11.250
Who were they?

00:09:11.340 --> 00:09:13.840
The other members of Team Autumn.
We separated from them.

00:09:14.260 --> 00:09:17.090
What is up with the people in Team Autumn?

00:09:17.260 --> 00:09:18.430
They're all jerks.

00:09:18.800 --> 00:09:21.060
I'm sorry. Please forgive them.

00:09:21.390 --> 00:09:22.810
What should we do now, Ms. Hana?

00:09:23.310 --> 00:09:25.730
Well, it's a little less scary now,
thanks to them.

00:09:26.270 --> 00:09:27.520
Let's keep going.

00:09:29.610 --> 00:09:31.940
I won't let things go
according to their plan.

00:09:32.030 --> 00:09:35.530
I would rather bite my tongue and die
than get manipulated like this.

00:09:35.610 --> 00:09:38.160
Oh, it looks like an underground railway.

00:09:38.490 --> 00:09:40.160
Let's move, you two.

00:09:40.450 --> 00:09:42.540
If we follow this,
it should lead us somewhere.

00:09:49.040 --> 00:09:51.380
This isn't going to start moving, right?

00:09:51.800 --> 00:09:52.710
Probably.

00:09:58.050 --> 00:09:58.970
What is this place?

00:09:59.050 --> 00:10:01.050
This is likely a farm.

00:10:01.140 --> 00:10:02.140
A farm?

00:10:02.760 --> 00:10:03.720
Underground?

00:10:04.100 --> 00:10:06.600
That means this isn't
any ordinary facility.

00:10:07.480 --> 00:10:11.520
I suspect that this is
an evacuation shelter.

00:10:13.530 --> 00:10:14.940
This must be a depot.

00:10:15.530 --> 00:10:16.610
Akiwo?

00:10:16.690 --> 00:10:19.700
Yes, I think it might be possible.

00:10:20.620 --> 00:10:23.280
The electricity works.
I'm going to connect the wires.

00:10:23.700 --> 00:10:25.540
It's running on
an independent power source.

00:10:26.200 --> 00:10:28.120
Nuclear power? What do you think?

00:10:28.410 --> 00:10:31.710
I bet it's either geothermal
or hydro powered for safety reasons.

00:10:33.040 --> 00:10:36.210
If we find a computer somewhere,
let's power it up.

00:10:41.010 --> 00:10:42.260
It unlocked automatically.

00:10:42.550 --> 00:10:44.430
Then there must still be people inside.

00:10:44.810 --> 00:10:46.100
That would seem to be the case.

00:10:52.190 --> 00:10:54.730
Only some parts of the network are
coming back up.

00:10:55.190 --> 00:10:57.990
The folders are all empty,
and we can't transfer files.

00:10:58.570 --> 00:11:00.610
This must be the map of the facility.

00:11:02.820 --> 00:11:04.280
This is the central "Summer" block,

00:11:04.910 --> 00:11:06.370
and "Spring" is the living quarters.

00:11:07.040 --> 00:11:08.710
The "Autumn" block is farms and livestock,

00:11:09.040 --> 00:11:10.420
and "Winter" is the storage.

00:11:11.710 --> 00:11:13.250
Let's start with the storage block.

00:11:19.130 --> 00:11:23.010
I've long suspected
that there must be shelters somewhere.

00:11:23.760 --> 00:11:25.510
Weren't you told of any, Mr. Izayoi?

00:11:26.600 --> 00:11:30.770
I was told the 7SEEDS Project was
only an experimental last resort,

00:11:31.060 --> 00:11:33.980
and that other means of survival
had already been put into place.

00:11:35.150 --> 00:11:36.730
You were brave to participate.

00:11:37.110 --> 00:11:38.530
Did you volunteer?

00:11:39.110 --> 00:11:40.110
Yes.

00:11:40.990 --> 00:11:42.660
But I was not prepared for it.

00:11:43.280 --> 00:11:47.540
However, I'm doing my best
for the sake of my team now.

00:11:50.120 --> 00:11:51.040
Mr. Aramaki.

00:11:59.710 --> 00:12:00.920
He was holding something.

00:12:04.840 --> 00:12:06.180
Is it a diary?

00:12:06.260 --> 00:12:11.600
"I feel I should leave something behind,
so I wrote this journal."

00:12:12.350 --> 00:12:14.770
<i>Thanks for coming, everybody.</i>

00:12:17.480 --> 00:12:19.940
The Pete Brothers.
You must be Mark Ibaraki.

00:12:20.070 --> 00:12:21.030
That's me.

00:12:21.610 --> 00:12:23.030
Can you lie through your teeth?

00:12:23.110 --> 00:12:24.030
What?

00:12:24.110 --> 00:12:25.740
What do you need teeth for?

00:12:25.820 --> 00:12:27.530
I tell lies just fine without them.

00:12:27.620 --> 00:12:28.790
Shut your trap, Pete.

00:12:29.370 --> 00:12:31.790
I mean, can you keep up your act
until the very end?

00:12:32.370 --> 00:12:35.540
Of course! No matter the time or place,
if there's a job for me,

00:12:35.630 --> 00:12:37.710
I'll be ready and willing to do it.

00:12:38.800 --> 00:12:41.670
<i>That fellow only gave me
his name as "Takashi."</i>

00:12:42.340 --> 00:12:45.760
<i>The location he sent me to
was halfway between Osaka and Kyoto.</i>

00:12:46.260 --> 00:12:47.430
Maria Miki?

00:12:48.640 --> 00:12:50.180
Good morning.

00:12:50.260 --> 00:12:51.890
Are you another one of the performers?

00:12:52.180 --> 00:12:53.690
Oh, I think so.

00:12:54.850 --> 00:12:56.980
<i>In the train to our destination,</i>

00:12:57.270 --> 00:13:00.780
<i>there were many happy families
and couples.</i>

00:13:00.860 --> 00:13:04.280
All I was told was that
this is an opening celebration

00:13:04.360 --> 00:13:06.200
for a new theme park.

00:13:08.370 --> 00:13:10.870
What a coincidence.
I've got one here, too.

00:13:10.950 --> 00:13:12.120
I'm not a dog.

00:13:12.200 --> 00:13:14.500
A ventriloquist? Wow!

00:13:14.580 --> 00:13:16.170
I'm really looking forward to your show.

00:13:16.620 --> 00:13:19.630
<i>We'll soon be arriving at Ryugu,
the final stop.</i>

00:13:20.750 --> 00:13:22.460
<i>Welcome to Ryugu.</i>

00:13:22.960 --> 00:13:26.510
<i>This giant facility is like
an underground land of dreams,</i>

00:13:26.970 --> 00:13:31.390
<i>filled with numerous shows
and attractions for your enjoyment.</i>

00:13:31.470 --> 00:13:37.400
My name is Michitsuna Oda.
I am the manager. I will look after you.

00:13:37.980 --> 00:13:39.900
<i>In addition to Maria and myself,</i>

00:13:40.190 --> 00:13:45.450
<i>the baseball star Hino, the DJ Kagami,
the visual effects artist Saruwatari,</i>

00:13:45.700 --> 00:13:48.660
<i>and the comic artist Sakata,
had all been recruited by Mr. Takashi.</i>

00:13:49.450 --> 00:13:51.160
<i>The audience didn't know a thing.</i>

00:13:51.240 --> 00:13:56.910
<i>We were all fooled into believing we were
lucky to be invited to a free event.</i>

00:14:02.210 --> 00:14:03.210
It's an earthquake.

00:14:03.300 --> 00:14:04.630
But something feels odd about it.

00:14:04.710 --> 00:14:07.130
Kagami, explain the situation
to our guests.

00:14:07.470 --> 00:14:09.640
Wait, what am I supposed to say?

00:14:10.220 --> 00:14:15.020
When I asked if you could stay calm
and talk through any situation,

00:14:15.480 --> 00:14:17.140
you said you could.

00:14:21.860 --> 00:14:27.150
We will now play news reports
from the surface on the big screen.

00:14:27.240 --> 00:14:32.620
<i>Everyone, please sit down and hold
your loved ones' hands as you watch.</i>

00:14:34.790 --> 00:14:36.330
<i>This morning, just past 3 a.m.,</i>

00:14:36.580 --> 00:14:40.170
<i>a meteorite dozens of kilometers
in diameter struck the Earth.</i>

00:14:41.130 --> 00:14:45.300
<i>Later, meteorites of various sizes
also rained down around the world.</i>

00:14:45.380 --> 00:14:48.260
<i>Even now, they continue to fall.</i>

00:14:48.880 --> 00:14:50.760
-No way!
-I can't believe this!

00:14:51.050 --> 00:14:52.430
What are we going to do?

00:14:52.600 --> 00:14:57.390
<i>At that time, we all thought
that was the end of everything.</i>

00:14:58.390 --> 00:15:00.140
<i>That we would all die in despair.</i>

00:15:02.860 --> 00:15:08.490
<i>Even if you feel alone</i>

00:15:09.200 --> 00:15:13.570
<i>Swallowed by the vast darkness</i>

00:15:14.160 --> 00:15:17.370
<i>Never forget that right now</i>

00:15:17.450 --> 00:15:23.920
<i>You are a shining light</i>

00:15:24.000 --> 00:15:26.170
<i>But Maria saved us.</i>

00:15:29.510 --> 00:15:31.630
<i>For all the dry and splintered hearts
In this world</i>

00:15:31.720 --> 00:15:33.300
<i>Before I knew it,</i>

00:15:33.390 --> 00:15:35.180
<i>everyone was quietly weeping.</i>

00:15:36.680 --> 00:15:38.770
Mommy!

00:15:39.180 --> 00:15:42.850
Are you lost, kids?
That's okay, I'll help find your mommy.

00:15:43.560 --> 00:15:46.610
<i>It was then we realized
the role we were meant to play.</i>

00:15:47.280 --> 00:15:51.110
<i>All the guests soon found
their own jobs to do.</i>

00:15:51.990 --> 00:15:54.660
<i>It seemed as if everything
was running smoothly.</i>

00:15:56.530 --> 00:15:58.200
Is this your family, Mr. Takashi?

00:15:58.700 --> 00:15:59.660
Yes.

00:15:59.950 --> 00:16:02.170
My wife works in the storage block here.

00:16:02.500 --> 00:16:03.620
What about this girl?

00:16:04.000 --> 00:16:05.590
My daughter is elsewhere.

00:16:05.750 --> 00:16:06.630
Why?

00:16:07.880 --> 00:16:09.840
All gates are narrow.

00:16:09.920 --> 00:16:10.880
What?

00:16:11.510 --> 00:16:13.680
Sorry, it's a phrase my wife uses.

00:16:14.510 --> 00:16:18.890
"The gates to heaven are narrow.
Two cannot pass through side by side."

00:16:20.180 --> 00:16:24.900
I told her that I intended to pass through
with her, which she seemed to like.

00:16:25.610 --> 00:16:28.150
Boy, you can't get more
lovey-dovey than that!

00:16:29.230 --> 00:16:30.360
I suppose so.

00:16:31.530 --> 00:16:35.070
<i>However, it wasn't long before
I came to truly understand that phrase.</i>

00:16:37.120 --> 00:16:38.620
<i>One day, there was an accident,</i>

00:16:38.870 --> 00:16:41.830
<i>and three-quarters of the storage block
became flooded with seawater.</i>

00:16:42.500 --> 00:16:46.630
<i>Mr. Takashi's wife died
trying to prevent that from happening.</i>

00:16:48.040 --> 00:16:51.800
<i>Ultimately, he was unable
to be with her in death.</i>

00:16:52.970 --> 00:16:57.180
<i>In the end,
we all must face death on our own.</i>

00:16:57.720 --> 00:17:00.310
<i>That's what she meant by "narrow gates."</i>

00:17:00.390 --> 00:17:02.980
<i>Due to the accident,
we no longer have enough food,</i>

00:17:03.180 --> 00:17:07.100
<i>so half of the people here
will move to another shelter.</i>

00:17:07.610 --> 00:17:10.900
<i>Like a stone rolling down a hill,
something big went into motion.</i>

00:17:10.980 --> 00:17:13.400
What? What do you mean
there's no Ise Shelter?

00:17:13.490 --> 00:17:16.700
Right, I thought we got videos
from them every morning.

00:17:17.070 --> 00:17:19.950
Actually, that's just footage I created.

00:17:20.450 --> 00:17:23.960
So what happened to
all the people who moved?

00:17:24.040 --> 00:17:25.460
There were thousands of them.

00:17:25.710 --> 00:17:26.750
Do you want to know?

00:17:27.750 --> 00:17:32.460
Then get on the train
headed for the Ise Shelter tomorrow.

00:17:34.510 --> 00:17:37.430
<i>I ended up being the one who went.</i>

00:17:38.140 --> 00:17:40.680
<i>During the ride, I put on my performance,</i>

00:17:40.760 --> 00:17:44.520
<i>and eventually, we stopped
at a small station to change trains.</i>

00:17:45.020 --> 00:17:46.020
YOU'RE ONLY GOING THIS FAR

00:17:46.100 --> 00:17:46.940
Oh, really?

00:17:47.020 --> 00:17:48.770
-Well, have a good trip, everyone.
-Thank you.

00:17:48.850 --> 00:17:50.190
-We'll miss you.
-Brush your teeth!

00:17:50.770 --> 00:17:53.280
I thought I was going with them
all the way to Ise.

00:17:54.190 --> 00:17:55.030
Mr. Takashi?

00:17:55.110 --> 00:17:55.990
This is Ise.

00:18:01.200 --> 00:18:02.790
What are they doing?

00:18:03.240 --> 00:18:05.200
We need all the resources we can get.

00:18:26.350 --> 00:18:28.350
It's for fertilizer and feed.

00:18:28.440 --> 00:18:30.860
What gives you the right...

00:18:31.650 --> 00:18:34.360
to decide who lives and who dies?

00:18:35.190 --> 00:18:37.860
The gates of heaven are narrow, Mark.

00:18:38.650 --> 00:18:41.620
Would you prefer that
we all happily starve to death?

00:18:41.700 --> 00:18:42.780
Then...

00:18:43.450 --> 00:18:46.950
Then why didn't you start
by getting rid of yourselves?

00:18:48.830 --> 00:18:52.080
It will eventually come to that,
unless the outside becomes habitable.

00:18:53.000 --> 00:18:55.800
Are you going to kill us, too?

00:18:56.550 --> 00:18:58.970
Me and even Maria?

00:18:59.050 --> 00:19:01.970
We still need you to keep working for us.

00:19:02.600 --> 00:19:05.430
You can keep lying until the very end.
Isn't that what you told me?

00:19:11.730 --> 00:19:14.230
Hey, it's Pi-suke and Mark!
Oh, and Pete too.

00:19:14.320 --> 00:19:16.360
Hey! I'm not some kind of sidekick.

00:19:17.070 --> 00:19:19.450
<i>I became an accomplice.</i>

00:19:19.530 --> 00:19:21.530
What should I do?

00:19:22.030 --> 00:19:24.950
I can't see them off with a smile anymore.

00:19:25.530 --> 00:19:27.870
Deep down, I've always been depressed.

00:19:29.160 --> 00:19:31.710
I don't think I can keep going like this.

00:19:32.040 --> 00:19:36.210
Mark, I don't believe that entertainers
need to be cheerful and happy

00:19:36.300 --> 00:19:37.840
even in their private lives.

00:19:38.630 --> 00:19:39.880
All they need is effort.

00:19:39.970 --> 00:19:43.340
That's what you've done all this time,
and you'll keep doing just that.

00:19:46.100 --> 00:19:51.190
Mr. Takashi also asked me if I could
keep singing until the very end.

00:19:52.980 --> 00:19:56.270
I told him that I would,
so long as someone was there to listen.

00:19:57.020 --> 00:20:02.200
Not because I like singing,
but because we are professionals.

00:20:04.740 --> 00:20:05.830
Let's do our best, Mark.

00:20:06.620 --> 00:20:10.370
Let's keep doing our jobs
until we reach the very end.

00:20:13.330 --> 00:20:15.000
It's almost Christmas.

00:20:15.500 --> 00:20:17.460
How about we put on a play with everyone?

00:20:17.590 --> 00:20:19.000
Like <i>A Christmas Carol</i> or something.

00:20:19.880 --> 00:20:20.970
That's a great idea.

00:20:21.050 --> 00:20:24.050
<i>But we were never able to
make that happen.</i>

00:20:24.470 --> 00:20:29.350
<i>A new threat reared its ugly head,
ready to put the final nail in our coffin.</i>

00:20:29.930 --> 00:20:30.930
<i>Tick X.</i>

00:20:31.020 --> 00:20:34.600
<i>The livestock that caught it
were incinerated.</i>

00:20:35.600 --> 00:20:37.570
<i>But it didn't end there.</i>

00:20:37.980 --> 00:20:39.940
<i>We realized it could
infect humans as well.</i>

00:20:40.940 --> 00:20:45.870
<i>We were trapped, faced with the fear
of something totally unknown.</i>

00:20:48.160 --> 00:20:53.080
So why are you called the Pete Brothers?
Pete is your older brother, right?

00:20:54.040 --> 00:20:56.380
Well, Pete really is my brother.

00:20:56.880 --> 00:20:58.790
Oh, but don't think I've gone crazy.

00:20:58.880 --> 00:21:01.460
This puppet used to belong
to my actual brother.

00:21:02.420 --> 00:21:05.890
My brother Mike was a few years
older than me,

00:21:05.970 --> 00:21:08.140
but he and my parents all died
in an accident.

00:21:08.220 --> 00:21:12.270
After that, I would always talk to Pete
as if he were my brother.

00:21:12.850 --> 00:21:14.190
I would talk for both of us.

00:21:14.480 --> 00:21:16.310
I was a really depressing kid.

00:21:16.690 --> 00:21:17.980
I was always getting bullied.

00:21:18.650 --> 00:21:21.940
One time, I had a chat with Pete
in front of everyone.

00:21:22.030 --> 00:21:23.360
They got a real kick out of it.

00:21:23.610 --> 00:21:25.200
And that's how it has been ever since.

00:21:25.280 --> 00:21:26.320
Right, Pete?

00:21:26.410 --> 00:21:28.990
Yes, that's our totally fake sob story.

00:21:29.070 --> 00:21:33.450
Mark, you don't need to force yourself
to be happy in front of me.

00:21:38.670 --> 00:21:40.380
We're going to quarantine this dog.

00:21:40.840 --> 00:21:44.630
It may have been infected by Tick X,
so we may need to dispose of it.

00:21:44.920 --> 00:21:45.760
Wait!

00:21:45.840 --> 00:21:47.800
You still have a job to do.

00:21:48.760 --> 00:21:49.640
Mr. Takashi!

00:21:54.140 --> 00:21:55.890
I shouldn't have brought it with me.

00:21:56.850 --> 00:22:00.440
I have a lot of regrets myself,
but I want you to know just one thing.

00:22:01.190 --> 00:22:06.450
The fact that you're here with me
makes me happy. It's the best thing ever!

00:22:06.820 --> 00:22:08.740
It makes everything else bearable!

00:22:18.210 --> 00:22:21.000
<i>I think we were really just afraid.</i>

00:22:25.460 --> 00:22:30.680
<i>The infection continued to spread,
and more people died every day.</i>

00:22:31.970 --> 00:22:33.220
I have something to ask of you.

00:22:33.510 --> 00:22:35.310
If survivors ever find us down here,

00:22:35.770 --> 00:22:39.980
we need to assure that Tick X
will never reach the surface.

00:22:40.060 --> 00:22:41.690
So I want you to seal off this shelter.

00:22:42.730 --> 00:22:45.280
I'm leaving to capture
the Minister who fled.

00:22:45.570 --> 00:22:48.780
After that, I want you
to close all access points.

00:22:48.860 --> 00:22:52.910
But when you come back,
it's okay to open one, right?

00:22:53.280 --> 00:22:54.620
That won't be necessary.

00:22:56.000 --> 00:22:59.870
We believe that Tick X can be stopped
if it is frozen.

00:23:00.620 --> 00:23:02.040
If it comes to that.

00:23:03.630 --> 00:23:06.260
Also, keep a close eye on Maria.

00:23:07.090 --> 00:23:10.010
If she does anything strange,
you'll have to stop her.

00:23:10.220 --> 00:23:11.220
I'm counting on you.

00:23:12.470 --> 00:23:15.680
Mark, keep fulfilling your role
until the very end.

00:23:18.140 --> 00:23:22.520
<i>I had also noticed that something
was wrong with Maria.</i>

00:23:24.730 --> 00:23:28.400
Just now, the electricity came on briefly
at one of the distant train stops.

00:23:29.240 --> 00:23:31.070
I wonder if that was Mr. Takashi's doing.

00:23:34.070 --> 00:23:36.620
<i>Mr. Takashi never came back.</i>

00:23:37.540 --> 00:23:40.790
<i>And we made sure all the doors
to the outside were shut tight.</i>

00:23:43.500 --> 00:23:44.380
Ouch!

00:23:45.000 --> 00:23:46.300
Was that one of the holding pins?

00:23:53.260 --> 00:23:55.970
-Maria. Your new costume is--
-Stay back!

00:23:56.720 --> 00:24:00.270
I was a hidden host.
It's my fault it spread to everyone.

00:24:01.020 --> 00:24:02.600
This filthy disease...

00:24:04.020 --> 00:24:05.150
Maria.

00:24:05.560 --> 00:24:07.520
Mark! Don't!

00:24:08.190 --> 00:24:10.440
It's fine, I've already got it too.

00:24:22.830 --> 00:24:24.500
Mark, I need your help.

00:24:24.880 --> 00:24:27.460
We need to bring an end
to this shelter, Ryugu.

00:24:29.420 --> 00:24:33.590
<i>I'll use my singing to draw out
the infected people who are in hiding.</i>

00:24:43.140 --> 00:24:45.310
<i>Your voice brings everyone to you.</i>

00:24:46.230 --> 00:24:47.820
<i>Because it gives us hope.</i>

00:24:49.150 --> 00:24:50.730
That's the last of them.

00:24:51.490 --> 00:24:53.610
Maria, everyone has come out now.

00:24:57.620 --> 00:25:00.540
I'm going, too.
It's been a pleasure working with you.

00:25:01.200 --> 00:25:02.450
Yes, same here.

00:25:18.430 --> 00:25:21.810
Mark, everyone is in the fridge.
Take care of the rest.

00:25:22.770 --> 00:25:23.730
Right.

00:25:41.830 --> 00:25:43.450
I've sealed the fridge shut.

00:25:43.540 --> 00:25:46.620
<i>I'll close the security door too,
so hurry back.</i>

00:25:52.340 --> 00:25:53.260
<i>Mark.</i>

00:25:53.880 --> 00:25:57.260
It was really great to meet all of you
and to work here together.

00:25:57.340 --> 00:25:59.300
<i>Yes. I enjoyed it, too.</i>

00:25:59.680 --> 00:26:01.310
Well, I'm heading off first.

00:26:09.810 --> 00:26:12.520
Mark, is this the end for us?

00:26:12.610 --> 00:26:15.940
Guess so. We had a long run together.

00:26:16.030 --> 00:26:18.360
So you're not going to
let me talk anymore?

00:26:22.530 --> 00:26:23.790
Big Bro...

00:26:23.870 --> 00:26:26.960
Nobody is watching.
Bawl your eyes out, kid.

00:26:31.460 --> 00:26:33.550
All right. Stay cool, Pete.

00:26:34.300 --> 00:26:35.960
Isn't that a weird way to say goodbye?

00:26:38.840 --> 00:26:40.390
I'm a Buddhist, though!

00:26:46.850 --> 00:26:49.480
<i>I think we really did a great job.</i>

00:26:49.810 --> 00:26:53.730
<i>Everyone did their best just to live,
and they were all honorable folks.</i>

00:26:54.780 --> 00:26:56.650
<i>I think I was an honorable guy, too.</i>

00:26:57.240 --> 00:27:00.570
<i>Last of all, I just want to say
that you shouldn't pity us.</i>

00:27:01.110 --> 00:27:05.410
<i>We lived, we laughed, and we stayed with
the people who meant the most to us.</i>

00:27:05.990 --> 00:27:09.210
"So we weren't miserable, we were..."

00:27:09.620 --> 00:27:11.040
I can't make out the rest.

00:27:16.380 --> 00:27:17.380
Ms. Hana?

00:27:21.800 --> 00:27:23.800
My father... Why is his photo here?

00:27:24.890 --> 00:27:27.060
So who unlocked that door?

00:27:31.600 --> 00:27:34.270
What the hell is this?

00:27:34.980 --> 00:27:36.820
Hurry up and get out!

00:27:36.900 --> 00:27:40.950
There's a flesh-eating disease
that was frozen here.

00:27:41.450 --> 00:27:42.910
Don't feed us your fairy tale--

00:27:42.990 --> 00:27:44.530
Cut the crap! Get out of there!

00:27:45.740 --> 00:27:47.240
When did you start giving orders?

00:27:47.910 --> 00:27:50.460
We're leaving. This place is creepy.

00:27:51.670 --> 00:27:54.540
I didn't think you were even capable
of acting like that.

00:28:02.430 --> 00:28:06.010
Well, it's good that all of those people
didn't die in vain.

00:28:06.640 --> 00:28:07.890
I wonder.

00:28:08.270 --> 00:28:12.850
Freezing may have just preserved it
so it could attack again in the future.

00:28:12.940 --> 00:28:14.900
That Maria was the culprit, after all.

00:28:15.440 --> 00:28:16.480
How dare you belittle them!

00:28:16.570 --> 00:28:21.490
But that was a waste too,
because nobody will ever come here again.

00:28:22.070 --> 00:28:26.370
I don't believe the death of others
can be summed up so simply.

00:28:27.490 --> 00:28:31.370
They died while fighting hard to survive,
so it's shameful to call it a waste.

00:28:34.830 --> 00:28:36.000
Haru.

00:28:36.080 --> 00:28:38.340
Welcome back. You sure took long.

00:28:39.920 --> 00:28:40.880
What's wrong?

00:28:53.440 --> 00:28:56.020
Is that a rescue team? A search party?

00:28:59.730 --> 00:29:02.110
You came from the underground.
What's down there?

00:29:02.440 --> 00:29:04.240
We found a shelter.

00:29:04.740 --> 00:29:06.870
One of the residents left this diary.

00:29:11.120 --> 00:29:13.000
What's the situation inside the shelter?

00:29:13.080 --> 00:29:14.540
Just like the diary says.

00:29:14.870 --> 00:29:17.500
The electricity is on,
but everyone is dead.

00:29:17.580 --> 00:29:19.090
Did you open the fridge?

00:29:19.170 --> 00:29:21.760
Yes. There were a ton of frozen bodies.

00:29:21.840 --> 00:29:25.090
You came out here without thinking
about the risk of infection?
